WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:04.930
Multi-platinum L.A. rockers Motley Crue rose
to fame with their debauchery-fueled brand

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:09.349
of heavy metal then enthralled readers around
the world with The Dirt, a hit memoir that

00:00:09.349 --> 00:00:11.740
made its way to Netflix in 2019.

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:15.560
Here's all the dirt behind The Dirt on the
page and on the screen.

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:19.990
Compared to other rock biography books, The
Dirt just has a lot… more.

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:23.970
It's got more stories of illegal activity
and heavy metal debauchery, and it's also

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:25.439
got a lot of authors.

00:00:25.439 --> 00:00:30.170
Five guys are credited on the cover: Mötley
Crüe band members Vince Neil, Nikki Sixx,

00:00:30.170 --> 00:00:33.440
Mick Mars, and Tommy Lee, along with Neil
Strauss.

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:37.100
He's the guy who did all the interviewing
and assembled a coherent narrative out of

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:41.570
what must have been stacks of tapes and copious
notes culled from following around four guys

00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:42.570
for months.

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:46.950
Strauss first hooked up with Mötley Crüe
in 1998, when he briefly toured with the band

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:48.629
for a profile in Spin.

00:00:48.629 --> 00:00:53.440
At the time, he was finalizing his Marilyn
Manson book, The Long Hard Road Out of Hell,

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:55.190
and the Crüe members that read it loved it.

00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:59.199
When publisher HarperCollins contracted the
group for a book later on, they immediately

00:00:59.199 --> 00:01:01.350
asked Strauss to helm the project.

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:05.720
Once more, he went on tour with Mötley Crüe
and visited each musician at home.

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:09.200
Probably the hardest part for him to write:
Vince Neil's account of his four-year-old

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:11.970
daughter's ultimately tragic battle with cancer.

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:13.040
Strauss told MTV:

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:14.880
"He was crying, I was crying.

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:15.950
It tore me apart.

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:19.200
When I gave him the book, I said, 'You might
not want to read this."

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:24.590
The Dirt manages to top most every other rock
biography with its sheer volume of nauseating

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:26.780
and unbelievable tall tales.

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:31.229
While Hammer of the Gods breathlessly describes
every despicable and disgusting act ever committed

00:01:31.229 --> 00:01:36.091
by the members of Led Zeppelin, it can't compete
with Vince Neil's egg burrito story from The

00:01:36.091 --> 00:01:37.091
Dirt.

00:01:37.091 --> 00:01:41.430
The Mötley Crüe singer discusses the band's
good ol' days, its early years as a band playing

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:46.369
L.A.'s Rainbow club by night, and using drummer
Tommy Lee's Chevy van as a "party truck" later

00:01:46.369 --> 00:01:47.369
at night.

00:01:47.369 --> 00:01:50.579
"Whoo, so this is the party truck, huh?

00:01:50.579 --> 00:01:53.049
You found it."

00:01:53.049 --> 00:01:54.049
Said Vince Neil:

00:01:54.049 --> 00:01:57.750
"After the Rainbow each night, we'd walk to
Santa Monica Boulevard, where all the young

00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:00.939
rockers and actors who never made it were
pimping themselves."

00:02:00.939 --> 00:02:04.969
And then, even if they had girlfriends at
the time, they'd entertain any girls who wanted

00:02:04.969 --> 00:02:06.549
to get into Tommy's van.

00:02:06.549 --> 00:02:10.450
Afterwards, the Crüe would scrounge up enough
money to buy a breakfast burrito from a local

00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:11.450
establishment.

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:15.540
Then, each member of the band would place
themselves, quote, "into the warm meat to

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:18.150
cover up the smell" of the women who had been
there earlier.

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:19.150
Why?

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:21.500
In their words: "So that our girlfriends didn't
know."

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:22.500
Oh.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:23.840
That...makes sense.

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:25.400
Uh...kinda.

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:30.000
While The Dirt reveals one brazen display
of Caligula-like excess after another, the

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.830
book goes a bit beyond the drug-taking and
exploits.

00:02:32.830 --> 00:02:37.510
For instance, in the mid-1980s, the band's
reps sought to protect its investment and

00:02:37.510 --> 00:02:40.650
made every member of the drink-and-drug-addled
group get clean.

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:42.140
Guitarist Mick Mars recounted:

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:46.740
"Our management was trying to do some kind
of extensive plastic surgery on the band,

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:50.850
and we had to see all these doctors who would
try to brainwash us into behaving differently.

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:54.400
Once a week we'd have to go to relationship
counseling like an old bitter married couple.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:58.530
There, we'd learn how to talk to each other
instead of fighting or we'd discuss our feelings

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:00.360
and whatever was going on that week."

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:05.300
Mars resented all that, especially since he'd
kicked his habits without any formal help,

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:07.560
but it was the waterworks that really irked
him.

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:08.560
He said:

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:11.830
"Every therapist wanted us to let loose and
cry, and I hate crybabies.

00:03:11.830 --> 00:03:15.530
I became so sick of seeing everyone in the
band bawling their eyes out."

00:03:15.530 --> 00:03:19.320
"You wanna know what rehab was really like?

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:21.400
Feelings and crying and apologies."

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:25.600
December 8, 1984 looms dark in rock history.

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:30.360
That's when an intoxicated Vince Neil drove
himself and Hanoi Rocks drummer Nicholas "Razzle"

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:34.150
Dingley to a liquor store to get more booze
for a multi-day party.

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:39.900
Neil lost control on a winding road and smashed
into another car, instantly killing his passenger.

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:44.250
The Dirt tells this horrific story, but it
also reveals that a few hours before that

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:48.240
tragedy, another sad and shocking event nearly
befell the group.

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:52.220
Neil says that he woke up that afternoon to
see something on the beach, which he described

00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:53.970
as "a small black whale."

00:03:53.970 --> 00:03:58.620
Upon examination, it was Mick Mars, "passed
out in the sand" in black leather from head

00:03:58.620 --> 00:03:59.620
to toe.

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:00.620
What did Neil do?

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:01.620
Nothing.

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:02.620
Neil wrote:

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:04.510
"He mumbled something about just wanting to
be left lying there.

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:07.560
so I left him there, looking like E.T. when
he was sick."

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:12.040
After dark, Mars regained consciousness…
but he was sure he was dead and was now a

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:15.740
ghost, and instinctively headed toward a "glow
of light coming from the distance."

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:19.500
It was Neil's house, where the party had turned
sad and silent.

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:20.500
Mars explained:

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:24.410
"I figured that they were crying for me: that
I had drowned in the water and they had discovered

00:04:24.410 --> 00:04:25.400
my body."

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:30.120
According to band manager Doug Thaler in The
Dirt, Vince Neil's self-sabotaging behavior

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:33.550
in the early '90s threatened his position
in Mötley Crüe.

00:04:33.550 --> 00:04:37.090
By February 1992, bassist Nikki Sixx was over
it.

00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:41.230
During a major rainstorm that flooded large
parts of L.A., each member made the treacherous

00:04:41.230 --> 00:04:42.710
journey to the studio.

00:04:42.710 --> 00:04:43.870
But not Neil.

00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:47.890
After waiting for Neil for hours, the band's
tour manager faxed the singer, telling him

00:04:47.890 --> 00:04:49.400
to show up ASAP.

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:53.330
Neil then called, explaining that he hadn't
done so already because the phone lines were

00:04:53.330 --> 00:04:54.330
down.

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:55.330
According to Sixx:

00:04:55.330 --> 00:04:58.810
"By the time he arrived the seed was already
planted in our minds that he was holding us

00:04:58.810 --> 00:04:59.810
back."

00:04:59.810 --> 00:05:02.130
Neil finally arrived, "soaked and sulking."

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:05.780
Sixx casually mentioned that the band was
considering "new lead singer talks," while

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:10.690
Neil retorted that the reason he skipped rehearsals
was because he thought the album was dumb.

00:05:10.690 --> 00:05:12.870
After more bickering, Neil left in a huff.

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:13.870
Sixx wrote:

00:05:13.870 --> 00:05:14.870
"He says he was fired.

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:15.800
I say he quit."

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:21.230
After the huge sales of the 1989 album Dr.
Feelgood, Elektra Records was eager to stay

00:05:21.230 --> 00:05:26.530
in the Mötley Crüe business, and in 1991
gave the band a $25 million contract… but

00:05:26.530 --> 00:05:28.610
the band's future remained shaky.

00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:31.660
With Neil gone, the group recruited singer
John Corabi.

00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:36.500
According to Thaler, the record the new Crüe
recorded took up "14 months and $2 million"

00:05:36.500 --> 00:05:37.810
in studio costs.

00:05:37.810 --> 00:05:39.880
All that for a relative dud.

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:44.650
Released in March 1994, Mötley Crüe debuted
at #7 on the Billboard 200.

00:05:44.650 --> 00:05:47.880
Then the album fell to 28 in its second week.

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:50.270
The supporting tour was a similar disaster.

00:05:50.270 --> 00:05:54.960
Nikki Sixx recalled that at the first stop
in Tucson, Arizona, "only 4,000 tickets had

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:57.530
been sold for a 15,000 seat amphitheater."

00:05:57.530 --> 00:06:01.760
He even tried to give away tickets at a radio
station, and only two people showed up.

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:02.760
What happened?

00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:06.530
Rock drastically changed between Dr. Feelgood
and 1994.

00:06:06.530 --> 00:06:11.040
The L.A. hair metal sound was out; grunge
and alternative rock were in.

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:16.360
Mötley Crüe was old news in the '90s, with
the low sales of Mötley Crüe surpassed by

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:22.270
even lower sales of its 1997 follow-up, the
Vince Neil reunion record Generation Swine.

00:06:22.270 --> 00:06:26.510
There was nowhere to go but up, and rather
than chase trends, Mötley Crüe embraced

00:06:26.510 --> 00:06:29.740
what fans wanted them to be degenerate party
monsters.

00:06:29.740 --> 00:06:34.310
And they did it not with an album, or tour,
or even a reality show they did it with The

00:06:34.310 --> 00:06:35.310
Dirt.

00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:39.300
Not only did literate music fans come to expect
a certain amount of sordid detail in rock

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:44.060
memoirs in the wake of The Dirt, but it spawned
a cottage industry in Mötley Crüe books.

00:06:44.060 --> 00:06:48.890
The success of The Dirt inspired Tommy Lee
and Vince Neil to go solo with their own individual

00:06:48.890 --> 00:06:53.530
memoirs, Tommyland and Tattoos &amp; Tequila,
respectively, while Nikki Sixx cleaned up

00:06:53.530 --> 00:06:58.080
the journals he wrote during the depths of
his drug addiction to create The Heroin Diaries.

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:01.520
Musicians take inspiration from wherever they
can.

00:07:01.530 --> 00:07:06.730
Nikki Sixx, always Mötley Crüe's most dominant
and prolific songwriter, took that idea to

00:07:06.730 --> 00:07:10.300
heart… even if things got a little too Inception-ey
along the way.

00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:15.400
The Dirt dredged up so many memories for Sixx
and his bandmates some bad, mostly good that

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:19.820
they got back together with departed drummer
Tommy Lee to record a brand new album with

00:07:19.820 --> 00:07:22.760
the original lineup for the first time in
more than a decade.

00:07:22.760 --> 00:07:25.560
That record: 2008's Saints of Los Angeles.

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:30.110
And while many Mötley Crüe songs are based
on actual experiences in Sixx's life, Saints

00:07:30.110 --> 00:07:32.340
is loosely based on The Dirt.

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:36.420
In other words, it's the musical version of
a book that's about… himself and the other

00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:37.840
members of Mötley Crüe.

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:41.690
In fact, the LP was almost actually named
The Dirt.

00:07:41.690 --> 00:07:43.980
"If that makes any sense.

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:48.960
It probably doesn't, but that's 'cuz I'm half
nuts anyway, so it's okay."

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:53.200
The Dirt first thrilled readers and rocked
its way to the New York Times bestseller list

00:07:53.220 --> 00:07:58.810
back in 2001… and the film version didn't
make its Netflix debut until 2019.

00:07:58.810 --> 00:08:02.930
That's a long time between source material
and adaptation, but it's not because nobody

00:08:02.930 --> 00:08:03.930
was trying.

00:08:03.930 --> 00:08:09.150
A film version of The Dirt could have hit
movie screens in late 2006 or early 2007.

00:08:09.150 --> 00:08:13.460
That's when Paramount Classics forged an agreement
to make the movie with MTV Films.

00:08:13.460 --> 00:08:18.190
Things got as far as a table read of the script
in 2014, but then Focus Features brought the

00:08:18.190 --> 00:08:23.470
project back from the dead in 2015, with Jackass
veteran Jeff Tremaine already attached as

00:08:23.470 --> 00:08:24.500
a director.

00:08:24.500 --> 00:08:28.030
Even once Netflix acquired the rights to the
movie version of The Dirt, it took a full

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:30.200
two years to get the thing finished.

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:32.720
Playing a rock star must be fun for actors.

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:37.089
They get to live out their wildest rock star
fantasies, and awards consideration is often

00:08:37.089 --> 00:08:38.580
part of the conversation.

00:08:38.580 --> 00:08:43.000
In the past two decades, a number of acclaimed
actors have won Oscars for portraying real-life

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:44.320
musical icons.

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:48.800
But when The Dirt finally got made, filmmakers
went with a cast made up largely of performers

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:54.260
who aren't quite household names, such as
Games of Thrones star Iwan Rheon as Mick Mars

00:08:54.260 --> 00:08:56.640
and Douglas Booth as Nikki Sixx.

00:08:56.640 --> 00:09:00.680
Over the years, a number of much bigger stars
were pursued or attached to the project.

00:09:00.680 --> 00:09:06.270
In 2007, Vince Neil announced at the opening
of his Las Vegas tattoo parlor that the legendary

00:09:06.270 --> 00:09:10.950
Christopher Walken had signed on to play the
legendary Ozzy Osbourne, while Val Kilmer

00:09:10.950 --> 00:09:13.680
was a go for a cameo as David Lee Roth.

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:16.040
None of those choices worked out, obviously.

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:20.870
In both the book and film adaptations of The
Dirt, cheating death is a major, recurring

00:09:20.870 --> 00:09:21.870
theme.

00:09:21.870 --> 00:09:25.800
Vince Neil walked away from a drunk driving
accident that killed his passenger, Mick Mars

00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:30.430
thought he died on a beach one day, and Nikki
Sixx recalled the time he actually was dead

00:09:30.430 --> 00:09:31.430
for a few minutes.

00:09:31.430 --> 00:09:35.630
It would seem that the theme extended to the
making of the film adaptation of The Dirt.

00:09:35.630 --> 00:09:40.540
During production in New Orleans in March
2018, a member of the film's crew was de-rigging

00:09:40.540 --> 00:09:44.100
a set when one of the electrical components
delivered a nasty shock.

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:46.540
A rep for the film told The Hollywood Reporter:

00:09:46.540 --> 00:09:51.430
"Even though all of our safety protocols were
in place, an accident did occur and a crewmember

00:09:51.430 --> 00:09:53.040
was injured by electricity."

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:57.080
Filming wasn't interrupted, because the cast
was off shooting somewhere else while this

00:09:57.080 --> 00:10:00.950
ill-fated de-rigger was tearing down a set
that was no longer needed.

00:10:00.950 --> 00:10:05.589
EMTs took him to a nearby hospital, where
he was listed in stable condition and recovered.

00:10:05.589 --> 00:10:09.720
No word on who'll be the next victim of the
Curse of the Crue...but you should probably

00:10:09.720 --> 00:10:13.790
stay far, far away from Tommy Lee just in
case.

00:10:13.790 --> 00:10:15.890
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:10:15.890 --> 00:10:19.730
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
musicians are coming soon.

